; demand_beside_recursion_1 -- a recursive SCC sitting BESIDE the demanded
; subgraph (test-matrix C4). Compiled with `-demand` (the .drflags sidecar,
; all four optimization modes -- demand is orthogonal to them, never a fifth
; mode).
;
; Two DISJOINT dataflow regions share the program:
;   * The demanded region: `pair(X, Y) : seed_2(X, Y)` consumed by the single
;     bound query `pair_of(bound X, free Y)`. The SIP walk from the bound
;     query propagates X backward through `pair` to `seed_2` and STOPS there.
;   * The beside region: a recursive transitive-closure SCC over `edge_2`
;     (`path`), published eagerly by the `path_out` message. Nothing in the
;     demand walk from `pair_of` ever reaches `path`/`edge_2`.
;
; THE PIN: the planner must NOT annex the recursive `path` SCC into the demand
; transform just because a recursion exists in the module. `path` has its own
; eager consumer (`path_out`), so it must materialize the FULL closure the
; instant its `edge_2` inputs arrive -- BEFORE (and independent of) any
; `pair_of` demand. The driver observes `path_out`'s published deltas eagerly
; after each edge batch (never having probed anything): an annexed-into-demand
; `path` would under-publish here. The demand transform still prunes `pair`
; to only the probed keys (answer-identical to the plain program, which the
; oracle referees).
;
; DELIBERATELY: one bound query, no all-free sibling, no negation/aggregate in
; either region (the single-adornment demand slice); `pair` is non-recursive
; (the demanded body is trivial -- the recursion is entirely beside it).

#message edge_2(u64 From, u64 To).
#message seed_2(u64 X, u64 Y).

#local path(u64 From, u64 To).
path(F, T) : edge_2(F, T).
path(F, T) : path(F, M), edge_2(M, T).

#message path_out(u64 From, u64 To) : path(From, To).

#local pair(u64 X, u64 Y).
pair(X, Y) : seed_2(X, Y).

#query pair_of(bound u64 X, free u64 Y) : pair(X, Y).
